# `just fmt` runs `prettier --write .` over the whole repository, and
# `just fmt-check` runs `prettier --check .` over it. Every entry below is a
# file prettier CAN parse and MUST NOT rewrite, with the reason it must not.
# Anything prettier cannot parse at all (`.rs`, `.sql`, `.sha256`, `.cstack`)
# is never selected in the first place and needs no entry — except where an
# entry is the guarantee rather than an observation, which is said so below.
#
# Prettier 3 reads `.gitignore` as well as this file, so generated trees
# (`dist/`, `storybook-static/`, `.e2e/`, `node_modules/`) are already out of
# scope and are deliberately not repeated here.
#
# THERE IS A SECOND IGNORE FILE: `examples/shop/.prettierignore`, for the
# `zen generate` output and `.next/`. It applies to that package's own `lint`
# script (`prettier --check`, run from that directory) and NOT to `just fmt`
# or `just fmt-check-web`, which run from here and read only this file. The
# two agree today, measured rather than assumed: every path it names is
# either in the root `.gitignore` already or a file prettier cannot parse
# (`examples/shop/zenstack/migrations/` holds `.sql` and a `.toml`, and
# `schema.zmodel` is not a language prettier knows). Add a `.ts`, `.json` or
# `.md` under one of those paths and they stop agreeing — the root run would
# check a generated file the package deliberately excludes. Mirror the entry
# here if that happens.

# Helm templates are Go templates that happen to have a `.yaml` extension: a
# line like `{{- include "vpay.validate" . -}}` is not YAML, prettier's YAML
# parser rejects it, and anything it did manage to reformat would break the
# indentation the templates depend on. `helm lint` is the formatter for this
# directory.
deploy/helm/**/templates/

# Fixture for
# `a_malformed_yaml_file_produces_a_clean_typed_error_not_a_panic`
# (`backends/crates/vpay-config/tests/fixtures/`). The unterminated quote is a
# genuine YAML syntax error: prettier cannot parse it, so `--write` leaves it
# alone but `--check` exits non-zero on it. That is the exp17 hazard
# (docs/plans/exp17-notes/opus-review.md finding 2), and the reason
# `just fmt-check` could not run prettier until this entry existed.
backends/crates/vpay-config/tests/fixtures/malformed.yml

# Generated by pnpm, in pnpm's own layout, and rewritten by pnpm on every
# install that resolves anything. Prettier's YAML printer produces a
# semantically identical but textually different file (measured 2026-09-10:
# +5444/-2837 lines, byte-different, parse-identical), so formatting it buys
# nothing and costs two things: `just fmt` stops being a no-op the moment
# anyone adds a dependency, and every dependency PR carries an eight-thousand
# line reformat that conflicts with every other branch in flight.
pnpm-lock.yaml

# `sqlx::migrate!` checksums the bytes of each migration, and
# `backends/migrations/MANIFEST.sha256` pins those checksums
# (`just verify-migrations`). A reflowed comment in a migration that has
# already shipped stops every database that applied the original from booting
# (issue #76, docs/runbooks/migrations.md). Prettier has no SQL parser today,
# so this entry changes nothing right now — it is here so the guarantee
# survives prettier gaining one, or someone adding a SQL plugin.
# `backends/migrations/README.md` is deliberately NOT ignored: it is prose,
# nothing hashes it, and it is already prettier-clean.
backends/migrations/*.sql

# Flutter/Dart tooling output (`sdks/flutter/*/build/`, `.dart_tool/`).
# Unlike every other generated tree named at the top of this file, these are
# NOT covered by the root `.gitignore`: git ignores them through the package's
# own nested file (`sdks/flutter/vpay_checkout_flutter/.gitignore`), and
# prettier reads only the ROOT `.gitignore`. So the "already out of scope"
# sentence above does not reach them, and an entry here is the guarantee
# rather than an observation.
#
# Measured 2026-09-14: after a `flutter test` and a `flutter build apk`,
# `just fmt-check-web` reported style issues in 150 files, of which 139 were
# generated JSON/manifests under these two trees and 12 were real source.
# CI never sees them — it runs no Flutter recipe (D-M3) — so this is a local
# papercut, not a CI failure, and it is real for anyone who builds the plugin.
sdks/flutter/**/build/
sdks/flutter/**/.dart_tool/

# Tauri plugin output (`sdks/tauri/tauri-plugin-vpay-checkout/`), for exactly
# the same reason as the Flutter trees above: git ignores these through the
# plugin's own nested `.gitignore`, prettier reads only the ROOT `.gitignore`,
# so the "already out of scope" sentence at the top of this file does not
# reach them and an entry here is the guarantee rather than an observation.
#
# `ios/.build/` is SwiftPM's build directory (it holds `.json` manifests
# prettier will happily rewrite), `android/build/` is Gradle's output for the
# plugin's library module, and `.tauri/` is the copy of Tauri's own
# Swift/Kotlin API the plugin's build extracts.
#
# Measured by Lane A2, 2026-09-22: `prettier --check` on this package failed
# on 10 files under `ios/.build/` and on two under `permissions/`. Re-measured
# by the docs lane the same day, after something had run Gradle in this tree:
# `pnpm exec prettier --check sdks/tauri` then flagged three more, all
# generated JSON under `android/build/intermediates/` — hence that entry.
#
# The crate's Rust build output needs NO entry, and that is an observation
# rather than a guarantee: `cargo metadata` on this crate reports its
# `target_directory` as the repository root's `target/`, which the root
# `.gitignore` already covers. Give this crate its own target directory and
# it will need a line here.
sdks/tauri/**/ios/.build/
sdks/tauri/**/android/build/
sdks/tauri/**/.tauri/
sdks/tauri/**/android/.tauri/

# The permission files below are the exception to the sentence above: they
# are TRACKED, not ignored by any `.gitignore`, and they are still generated
# — `tauri_plugin::Builder` in the crate's `build.rs` rewrites all of them on
# **every** `cargo build`, in its own layout, the way every plugin in
# `tauri-apps/plugins-workspace` commits them (a consuming app's build script
# discovers a plugin's permissions from them). Prettier CAN parse
# `autogenerated/reference.md` and `schemas/schema.json` and MUST NOT rewrite
# them: formatting buys one clean run and is undone by the next `cargo build`,
# which then leaves a dirty tree and a red `fmt-check-web` for whoever built
# the crate last.
#
# Measured by Lane A2, 2026-09-22: `prettier --check` fails on
# `permissions/autogenerated/reference.md` and `permissions/schemas/schema.json`
# as generated. The generator's output is the file's correct content.
sdks/tauri/**/permissions/autogenerated/
sdks/tauri/**/permissions/schemas/

# Generated by the Tauri CLI for the example app (`cargo tauri android init`
# / `ios init` write `src-tauri/gen/`, and the CLI then leaves it alone). The
# same guarantee-not-observation reasoning as the two blocks above: the tree
# is ignored by `examples/tauri-checkout/`'s own nested `.gitignore`, which
# prettier does not read.
#
# Added 2026-09-22 ahead of the tree existing, from the Tauri CLI's documented
# layout rather than from a directory anyone had looked at. **Confirmed the
# same day by Lane D, and the path is exactly right**: `tauri android init`
# and `tauri ios init` write a full Gradle project and a full Xcode project
# under this one directory, both carrying absolute paths to the machine that
# ran them and both holding build output (`jniLibs/**/*.so`, `Externals/`,
# `app/build/outputs/`). The whole tree is reproduced by re-running `init`,
# which is why the example ignores it wholesale instead of ignoring a dozen
# paths inside it the way `create-tauri-app`'s own templates do.
examples/tauri-checkout/src-tauri/gen/

# Agent worktrees (`.claude/worktrees/<name>/`), for the same reason as the
# Flutter trees above and with the same consequence: git does not ignore
# `.claude/` — `.claude/skills/vaam-ui` is tracked — so the root `.gitignore`
# does not put these out of scope, and each worktree is a FULL SECOND COPY of
# this repository. `prettier --check .` from the main checkout therefore walks
# every checked-out branch as well as this one, and the run grows with the
# number of worktrees rather than with the size of the tree.
#
# This is what made `just ci` time out in `fmt-check-web` on PR #172 while its
# author was verifying it. CI never sees it — a runner clones one tree and has
# no `.claude/worktrees/` — so it is a local papercut, not a CI failure, and
# it is real for anyone who uses `git worktree` under `.claude/`.
.claude/worktrees/

# Vendored agent skills (`.agents/skills/**`), installed by #188 from
# `cratestack/cratestack-skills` and pinned in `skills-lock.json` by a
# per-skill `computedHash`. Prettier CAN parse this markdown and MUST NOT
# rewrite it: reformatting a vendored copy changes the bytes the lock file
# hashes, so the next install or drift check would disagree with what is on
# disk, and the change would be reverted by the next sync anyway. Formatting
# is the upstream repository's business, not this one's.
#
# Measured 2026-09-16: `just fmt-check-web` flagged 29 files on master, of
# which 17 were these. `just ci` runs `fmt-check` first, so master's whole
# gate was red on vendored content nobody here should be rewriting.
.agents/skills/

# Written by release-please, in release-please's own style, on every release —
# `*` bullets rather than `-`, and a double blank line before each `###`
# heading. Prettier CAN parse this markdown and MUST NOT rewrite it: the
# changelog updater splices a NEW section into the file at each release and
# does not touch what is already there, so formatting the file buys one clean
# run and makes every future release pull request red on `fmt-check-web` — the
# one pull request nobody can repair by hand without taking the release apart.
#
# Measured 2026-09-17: `v0.1.1` (#203) created this file and `master`'s `web`
# job failed on it the same night. On that file prettier's rewrite is two
# bullet markers and two blank lines — small, and it would be re-earned in
# full by the section the NEXT release appends. Hand-editing the changelog is
# not the repair either: release-please owns the file, and an edit it did not
# make is an edit it will not preserve.
CHANGELOG.md
